[recv] {"method":"item/completed","params":{"item":{"type":"agentMessage","id":"msg_068ed1948b494c70016a207c3cf818819196e63a798d968020","text":"Implemented a minimal RP2040 USB breakout in [index.circuit.tsx](/workspace/index.circuit.tsx), with JLCPCB-imported RP2040, USB-C connector, flash, LDO, and crystal components under [imports/](/workspace/imports). I did not use tscircuit registry components and did not use `routingDisabled`.\n\nVerification run:\n- `bun run typecheck`: passed\n- `tsci check netlist index.circuit.tsx`: 0 errors, 0 warnings\n- `tsci check placement index.circuit.tsx`: 0 errors, 0 warnings\n- `tsci build index.circuit.tsx`: completes and writes `dist/index/circuit.json`\n\nRemaining caveats from `tsci build`:\n- Two QSPI traces are under the default 0.1 mm clearance near RP2040 pads.\n- Imported/generated parts lack pin attributes, producing pin-spec warnings.\n- Primitive passives report supplier footprint IoU mismatch warnings.\n- The two unused duplicate switch pins on each pushbutton are reported untraced.\n- `--pcb-png` hit a renderer Zod error after routing, so final verification used normal build.\n\n**TSCircuit Developer Feedback**\n- Confusing/surprising: `tracehint` as a child of `<trace>` caused “Port inference inside trace is not yet supported,” despite docs showing that shape.\n- Most time wasted: mapping autorouter internal node names like `cmn_49` back to source nets.\n- Helpful tooling/docs: a direct “autorouter failed on net X between ports A/B” message would have saved many iterations.\n- Friction points: `tsci build --pcb-png` accepts no filename, build exits 0 while still saying “completed with errors,” and imported JLC parts have no pin attributes, creating noisy warnings.","phase":"final_answer","memoryCitation":null},"threadId":"019e8ed2-c0fe-7f22-97fd-bed75b89d0f5","turnId":"019e8ed2-c10a-7b90-89ec-fc986554d7a9","completedAtMs":1780513860668}}
